// Everything without a builder answers a `Mutable<T>` cell, the mutable counterpart of a single
// value.
//
// **A string was in this half until 2026-10-23 and is not any more.** The reason it was is worth
// keeping, because it is the sentence that was overturned: "a MutableString is *filled* rather than
// converted and so is not this conversion". The distinction did not survive -- a MutableArray is
// filled too, `push` being the only way into one, and `mutable([1, 2])` converts anyway. What
// settled it is that the old answer *compiled*: `mutable("hi")` matched the bare `T` and the
// complaint arrived at whatever touched the cell, pointing at a line that was not the mistake.
// `mutable(s)` is text's builder now, and string.zm carries the account.
//
// A builder is still in this half, and it answers a cell holding that same handle rather than a
// clone of it -- pushing through what comes back out changes the original, which is what the last
// two lines check, and which is now true of a MutableString argument as well. There is no way to
// clone a builder at all: `mutable_array_from` and its two siblings were removed once `mutable`
// covered the conversion they were mostly used for.
fn main() {
    val ci = mutable(7)
    set<int>(ci, 8)
    print(get<int>(ci))

    print(get<bool>(mutable(true)))

    val ab = mutable<int>([1, 2])
    val held = get<MutableArray<int>>(mutable(ab))
    push(held, 3)
    print(snapshot(ab))

    // the same of text's builder, which `mutable(s)` now produces but `mutable(sb)` still wraps
    val sb = mutable("hi")
    val held_sb = get<MutableString>(mutable(sb))
    append(held_sb, " there")
    print(snapshot(sb))
}
